  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nExpect fairly calm weather throughout the period, even though some \nrain and thunderstorm chances will move through the area from time \nto time. The rain chances could even come as early as this afternoon \nas high pressure ridging in the lower levels peaks today. While this \nwill keep mostly sunny skies overhead, given that some diurnal \ninstability will be created by the afternoon hours and the ridging \nwill allow the Lake Superior and Lake Michigan lake breezes to move \ninland, there could be a few pop-up showers and thunderstorms that \ndevelop in the interior west and central as soon as early this \nafternoon, potentially continuing on until early this evening. Thus, \nwhile some CAMs do show us dry still for today, there is up to \naround a 30% chance that some showers and garden-variety \nthunderstorms move over some of the interior west and central this \nafternoon. Thankfully, with the ridging bringing limited wind shear \nover the area, no severe weather is expected today. \n\nWith the ridging cresting today and leaving starting on Thursday, \nexpect warm air advection to continue over da Yoop today into \nThursday. Thus, expect temperatures to climb into the upper 70s \nalong the lakeshore to mid 80s inland today, and into the 80s to \npotentially even around 90 in a few spots over the interior west and \nalong the southerly downslopes along Lake Superior on Thursday; be \nsure to stay hydrated if you're working outside today and Thursday. \n\nA shortwave low lifting off of the Northern Rockies Wednesday night \nis looking to potentially bring additional showers and non-severe \nthunderstorms back across the area late Thursday through Saturday. \nWhile this low is looking to miss us well to the south, because it \nis also looking to interact with the cold front of a low passing \nover Hudson Bay at around the same time, we could see some garden-\nvariety convection as well as some synoptic-scale light rainfall \nduring the late-week. Again, no severe weather is expected as there \nlooks to be very little wind shear within the atmosphere to work \nwith over the Yoop during this nearly 3-day stretch. While certainly \nnot guaranteed, the NBM has around a 40-75% chance across the entire \nYoop that at least a wetting (quarter inch or greater) rainfall will \nbe seen within a 24-hour period late Thursday through Saturday, with \nthe greatest probabilities being seen in the west where the driest \nground conditions are; hopefully the rainfall will make a dent in \nthe drought conditions observed in that part of Upper MI.\n\nMore high pressure ridging moves in this weekend as temperatures \ncloser to normal are seen behind the low. However, don't be \nsurprised if we see a near rinse-and-repeat of what we see for the \nlast half of this week as another shortwave low could bring \nadditional shower and thunderstorm chances to the area near the end \nof the period (early to mid-next week).",
